        {
          "path": "_m/s/w/b/b/artemision-bronze-wp/bronze-statue-of-zeus-or-posei-1.jpg",
          "filename": "Bronze statue of Zeus or Poseidon from Artemision, Euboea. ca. 460 BC. (3).jpg",
          "caption": "Bronze statue of a god, found at the bottom of the sea off cape Artemision, in north Euboea. The god, shown in great stride, extends his left arm forward, while throwing the thunderbolt or the trident, which he held in his right hand. The identification as Zeus or Poseidon is controversial (the former seems more probable). The bronze statue is one of the few preserved original works of the Severe Style, notable for the exquisite rendering of motion and anatomy. It is certainly the work of a great sculptor of the early Classical period. Height 2,09 m. Accession number: X 15161. National Archaeological Museum of Athens. Athens, Greece. Text CREDIT: Website of the National Archaeological Museum of Athens",
          "alt": "Bronze statue of a god, found at the bottom of the sea off cape Artemision, in north Euboea. The god, shown in great stride, extends his left arm forward, while throwing the thunderbolt or the trident, which he held in his right hand. The identification as Zeus or Poseidon is controversial (the former seems more probable). The bronze statue is one of the few preserved original works of the Severe Style, notable for the exquisite rendering of motion and anatomy. It is certainly the work of a great sculptor of the early Classical period. Height 2,09 m. Accession number: X 15161. National Archaeological Museum of Athens. Athens, Greece. Text CREDIT: Website of the National Archaeological Museum of Athens",
          "width": 4000,
          "height": 6000,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
          "artist": "George E. Koronaios",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_statue_of_Zeus_or_Poseidon_from_Artemision,_Euboea._ca._460_BC._(3).jpg",
